1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,730
The brush uncovers two sheets of copper, joined by a ring and engraved on the inside.

2
00:00:05,730 --> 00:00:11,815
This is a legal deed — a grant of land, recorded in metal so it would outlast everybody who signed it.

3
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:22,699
Hundreds of these survive, and they are the single most useful source for how early Indian society was changing.

4
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:27,879
Inscriptions, especially the copper plates.

5
00:00:27,879 --> 00:00:33,203
Texts, including law books, which tell us what was supposed to happen rather than what did.

6
00:00:33,203 --> 00:00:33,955
And Fa-Hien,

7
00:00:33,955 --> 00:00:37,947
a Chinese Buddhist pilgrim who travelled in India in the early fifth

8
00:00:37,947 --> 00:00:42,182
century looking for Buddhist texts and wrote down what he observed.

9
00:00:44,467 --> 00:00:48,508
The structure, briefly, because questions ask for the terms.

10
00:00:48,508 --> 00:00:53,610
The empire divided into provinces, bhuktis, under governors called uparikas.

11
00:00:53,610 --> 00:00:57,620
Provinces into districts, vishayas, under vishayapatis.

12
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:02,815
And below that the village, with its headman and local assemblies.

13
00:01:02,815 --> 00:01:05,836
Compared with the Mauryas this is a looser arrangement:

14
00:01:05,836 --> 00:01:12,233
more was left to local people and to feudatories who acknowledged the Guptas without being administered by them.

15
00:01:15,567 --> 00:01:17,467
Now the change that matters most.

16
00:01:17,467 --> 00:01:21,492
The Guptas and their successors granted land — to Brahmins, to temples,

17
00:01:21,492 --> 00:01:25,482
to monasteries, and increasingly to officials in place of salary.

18
00:01:29,767 --> 00:01:37,336
The grant usually transferred the revenue of a village, and often the right to administer it, sometimes in perpetuity and free of tax.

19
00:01:37,336 --> 00:01:40,307
Follow that through and you can see the consequence:

20
00:01:40,307 --> 00:01:45,990
every grant is a piece of the state's income and authority handed permanently to somebody else.

21
00:01:49,033 --> 00:01:52,575
Fa-Hien describes the Ganga plains as peaceful and prosperous.

22
00:01:52,575 --> 00:01:57,466
He notes that travel was safe, that charitable rest houses and free hospitals existed,

23
00:01:57,466 --> 00:02:00,971
that officials did not demand documents from travellers.

24
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:04,734
He also notes, in passing,

25
00:02:04,734 --> 00:02:08,568
that people called chandalas lived outside the towns and had to stri

26
00:02:08,568 --> 00:02:12,458
ke a piece of wood to announce themselves so others could avoid them.

27
00:02:12,458 --> 00:02:16,926
Both observations are in the same account, and an honest answer uses both.

28
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:27,154
What else the sources show: guilds remained important in towns; villages ran a good deal of their own affairs;

29
00:02:27,154 --> 00:02:31,109
and the law books of this period set out a social order of great rigidity,

30
00:02:31,109 --> 00:02:36,577
with the position of women and of the lowest-ranked groups more restricted than in earlier texts.

31
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:42,809
Remember the distinction — the law books are prescriptive.

32
00:02:42,809 --> 00:02:49,364
They tell you what their authors wanted, which is evidence of attitudes, not a description of how everybody lived.

33
00:02:53,133 --> 00:02:56,077
This is one of the big arguments in Indian history.

34
00:02:56,077 --> 00:03:05,756
One influential position holds that from the Gupta period land grants produced something properly called Indian feudalism — power and revenue devolving to intermediaries,

35
00:03:05,756 --> 00:03:11,263
peasants tied more tightly to the land, trade and towns declining, and the state weakening.

36
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:20,812
The other holds that the European comparison imports assumptions that do not fit,

37
00:03:20,812 --> 00:03:27,108
that the evidence for urban decline is mixed, and that what happened is better described as regional states forming.

38
00:03:27,108 --> 00:03:28,991
You are not expected to settle it.

39
00:03:28,991 --> 00:03:32,735
You are expected to know both sides and the evidence each uses.

40
00:03:37,267 --> 00:03:40,133
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

41
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:42,160
Land granted, power divided.

42
00:03:42,160 --> 00:03:45,605
Every plate gives a piece of the state away.

43
00:03:49,967 --> 00:03:50,757
Dig Quiz.

44
00:03:50,757 --> 00:03:52,258
Pause and name it.

45
00:03:52,258 --> 00:03:56,288
First: the Gupta province, governed by an uparika.

46
00:03:56,288 --> 00:03:57,236
The bhukti.

47
00:03:57,236 --> 00:04:02,513
Second: the engraved metal document recording a grant of land.

48
00:04:04,067 --> 00:04:05,421
A copper-plate grant.

49
00:04:05,421 --> 00:04:12,430
Third: the Chinese pilgrim who travelled in India in the early fifth century and described the Ganga plains.

50
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:19,670
Fa-Hien.

51
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:26,298
In Mains, a standard question asks whether the Gupta period saw the beginnings of Indian feudalism.

52
00:04:28,433 --> 00:04:34,169
Set out the land-grant evidence, give both positions fairly, and commit to one with reasons.

53
00:04:36,833 --> 00:04:43,660
A copper plate takes its place in the gallery: a deed written in metal because somebody meant it to be permanent, and it was.

54
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:53,643
Next gallery: the mathematics, the astronomy and the poetry — and whether this really was a golden age.

55
00:04:53,643 --> 00:04:57,565
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

